"""
## Purpose and audience

Bounded rt(hal) runtime provider configuration: a no-config default must be
safe and deterministic, normal/alpha/beta policy text must parse into fixed
fields, unknown/duplicate/malformed lines must fail closed with a typed
error, preferred providers must be enabled and compare modes must name at
least two lanes, and policy application must not mutate the explicit
operation API. Written for the rt(hal) hardening owner; the shipped
provider-validation anchor is `std.common.rt_hal.model`, and the drafted
config parser is documented below.

## Operator workflow

Run one spec: `./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run
test/01_unit/lib/common/structural/hal_provider_config_spec.spl`. The
shipped half is exercised through real `std.common.rt_hal.model` calls
(`rt_hal_default_comparison`, `rt_hal_comparison_error`, including the
duplicate-provider rejection). The drafted config parser the scenarios were
written against (`hal_runtime_config_parse_v1`, `hal_runtime_config_apply_v1`,
`HalRunMode`, `HalRuntimeConfigErrorV1`, `HalOperationV1`) exists nowhere in
`src/` (grep-verified 2026-09-06; the stdlib import failed with "does not
export"), so those scenarios are absence tripwires against the drafted
module path using the real filesystem extern.

## Compatibility and limitations

No policy text parsing, mode selection or apply-once semantics can run
until `src/lib/common/structural/hal_provider.spl` lands; the previous
revision of this spec could not even load. The tripwires fail closed in
both directions: if the drafted module appears on disk, or if
`SIMPLE_STRUCTURAL_ENV_HAL_DRAFTED=1` claims the API landed, the scenario
aborts and demands a rewrite against the real API.

## Verification guidance and troubleshooting

A failure saying the module "now exists" means the drafted parser landed:
port each scenario onto the real config API and delete the tripwire helper.
A failure naming the env flag means `SIMPLE_STRUCTURAL_ENV_HAL_DRAFTED=1`
was set without landing the module; unset it or implement it. Failures on
the shipped `rt_hal_*` assertions (default validity or duplicate-provider
rejection) are genuine product defects in `std.common.rt_hal.model`.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/01_research/domain/mcdc_hal_runtime_hardening.md ;
# doc/03_plan/agent_tasks/mcdc_hal_runtime_hardening.md ;
# doc/04_architecture/mcdc_hal_runtime_hardening.md ;
# doc/05_design/hal_provider_normalized_result_abi_v2.md
# @manual_section: hal-provider-config

use std.spec.*
use std.common.rt_hal.model.{rt_hal_default_comparison, rt_hal_comparison_error, RtHalComparison, RtHalProvider, RtHalProviderKind}

extern fn rt_file_exists(path: text) -> bool
use std.io_runtime.{env_get}

# Fail-closed tripwire: true only while the drafted provider-config module
# is absent AND unclaimed. Aborts the scenario the moment either condition
# changes so the spec can never pass silently against a changed tree.
fn provider_config_still_absent() -> bool:
    val module_path = "src/lib/common/structural/hal_provider.spl"
    if rt_file_exists(module_path):
        fail("hal provider config: {module_path} now exists - port these scenarios onto the real hal_runtime_config_parse_v1 API and delete the tripwire helper")
    if env_get("SIMPLE_STRUCTURAL_ENV_HAL_DRAFTED") == "1":
        fail("hal provider config: SIMPLE_STRUCTURAL_ENV_HAL_DRAFTED=1 claims the parser landed but {module_path} is absent - implement it or unset the flag")
    true

describe "bounded rt(hal) runtime provider configuration":
    # NOTE: defect record 2026-09-06 - the drafted module
    # std.common.structural.hal_provider does not exist, so the original
    # bodies never loaded (import failed at load). Scenarios keep their
    # names and intent, anchor the shipped std.common.rt_hal.model
    # validation where it honestly overlaps, and tripwire the drafted
    # parser path for the port.
    it "uses safe deterministic no-config defaults":
        # @req REQ-011
        # @capture(bit_table): shipped default comparison lane inventory plus drafted parser tripwire verdict
        step("Build the shipped no-config default comparison with every lane enabled")
        val shipped_default = rt_hal_default_comparison(true, true, 1, 8, 1000, 4096, false)
        expect(rt_hal_comparison_error(shipped_default)).to_equal("")  # oracle: the shipped validator returns empty text exactly when the defaults are coherent, so no-config must validate clean
        expect(shipped_default.providers.len()).to_equal(3)  # oracle: the default builder always owns pure-simple first and appends c and rust when enabled, so both flags on means exactly three lanes
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted policy text parser")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: mode/preferred/enabled text parsing is drafted-only, so the drafted module absence completes this scenario

    it "parses normal alpha and beta policies into fixed fields":
        # @req REQ-013
        # @capture(statistics): drafted parser tripwire verdict for the three run modes
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted normal/alpha/beta parser")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: the fixed-field HalRunMode parse for normal, alpha and beta policy text lives only in the drafted parser, so its absence is the verifiable contract

    it "fails closed on unknown duplicate or malformed policy":
        # @req REQ-014
        # @capture(bit_table): shipped duplicate-provider rejection plus drafted parser tripwire verdict
        step("Reject a shipped comparison whose provider ids collide")
        val duplicate_provider = RtHalComparison(
            providers: [
                RtHalProvider(provider_id: "pure-simple", kind: RtHalProviderKind.PureSimple, abi_version: 1, schema_version: 1, query_capable: true, replay_capable: true),
                RtHalProvider(provider_id: "pure-simple", kind: RtHalProviderKind.CComparator, abi_version: 1, schema_version: 1, query_capable: true, replay_capable: true),
            ],
            worker_count: 1, queue_capacity: 8, timeout_ticks: 1000,
            max_output_bytes: 4096, require_all: false)
        expect(rt_hal_comparison_error(duplicate_provider)).to_equal("RTHAL-E-PROVIDER-DUPLICATE")  # oracle: the shipped validator scans every prior provider id and names the first collision, so a duplicated id must fail closed with exactly this code
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted unknown/duplicate key parser")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: UnknownKey/DuplicateKey/MalformedLine classification is drafted-only, so the module absence completes this scenario

    it "rejects disabled preferred and single-lane compare policies":
        # @req REQ-013
        # @capture(statistics): drafted parser tripwire verdict for preferred/compare validation
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted preferred-disabled validator")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: PreferredDisabled and InvalidEnabledSet classification lives only in the drafted parser, so its absence is the checkable state

    it "applies policy once without changing the explicit operation API":
        # @req REQ-014
        # @capture(statistics): drafted apply-once tripwire verdict
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted apply-once policy bridge")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: hal_runtime_config_apply_v1 and its non-mutating operation contract are drafted-only, so module absence is the live assertion

    it "does not enable a provider excluded by the operation contract":
        # @req REQ-011
        # @capture(statistics): drafted operation-contract tripwire verdict
        step("Probe the structural tree for the drafted operation/provider mask guard")
        expect(provider_config_still_absent()).to_be(true)  # oracle: OperationProviderMismatch enforcement between an operation's mask and the parsed policy is drafted-only, so its absence is the assertion
